Plan §12 calls for 'tools bench' to measure tick cost, pathfinding,
and vision against plan §15's perf budgets. This commit lands the
tick-cost harness — the most important baseline for the Alpha
declaration (plan §15: '≤ 1 ms avg, ≤ 4 ms p99' on an Alpha-size match).

Implementation: a new bench subcommand drives N simulation steps on
the Alpha content (with or without AI controllers via --no-ai) and
samples each tick's wall-clock duration with std::time::Instant. The
samples are presentation-only telemetry — they live in tools and never
reach the simulation (FD-6 / the architecture-law scan exempts
crates/tools/).

Reports avg / p50 / p95 / p99 / max against the plan's thresholds,
plus throughput in ticks/s and end entity count (so the workload
shape is visible — 'Alpha-size ≤ 200' or '10x scale ≈ 2000' by
configuring the content).

Criterion integration (plan §12 mentions it) is the natural
follow-up; this harness gives M10 the same numbers criterion would,
without pulling criterion's transitive deps into the workspace's
build for every CI run.

Sample run on this commit (dev profile, --ticks 300 --no-ai):
  avg 0.40 ms, p99 0.47 ms, 2512 ticks/s — meets all three budgets.
The release-profile numbers (the ones plan §15 actually targets)
will be tighter; M10 records them as the pinned baselines.
